---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const updated = '2026-06-16';
---

<Base
  title="Launching Zone 8a Garden: One Bed, Three Crops, All Winter"
  description="An executive summary of zone8a.com: a documented high-rotation raised bed that carries a Zone 8a garden from spring greens into a winter brassica harvest."
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/`}>&larr; Home</a></p>
  <h1>Launching Zone 8a Garden</h1>
  <p><em>Published {updated}</em></p>

  <p>
    Zone 8a Garden is live at <a href="https://zone8a.com">zone8a.com</a>. I built it to
    document one bed, all the way through. Not a sprawling almanac, not a feed of
    disconnected tips. One 4x8 foot raised bed, run as a relay that grows three crops in a
    single year and keeps cutting greens through winter.
  </p>

  <h2>Where Zone 8a actually is</h2>
  <p>
    The whole site is keyed to USDA Zone 8a, so it helps to know where that is. On the
    current USDA Plant Hardiness Zone Map, the version updated in November 2023, Zone 8a is
    the band where the coldest night of an average year runs between 10 and 15 degrees
    Fahrenheit. That is a typical winter low, not a daily average. Mild, but a long way from
    frost-free.
  </p>
  <p>
    Geographically it falls across much of the southern United States: the coastal Carolinas,
    a wide stretch of Georgia, parts of Alabama, Mississippi, and Louisiana, and big sections
    of Texas. It turns up again out west, in inland and coastal parts of Oregon and
    Washington, and in patches of the higher desert Southwest. The 2023 update nudged a lot of
    these areas a half zone warmer than the old 2012 map, so if you last looked up your zone a
    decade ago, it is worth checking again. You might already be in 8a and not know it.
  </p>

  <figure class="zone-map">
    <img
      src={`${base}/zone-8a-map.svg`}
      width="959"
      height="593"
      loading="lazy"
      alt="Map of the contiguous United States with Zone 8a regions shaded. The deepest shade covers Georgia and South Carolina; a lighter shade covers North Carolina, Alabama, Mississippi, Louisiana, Arkansas, Texas, New Mexico, Arizona, California, Oregon, and Washington."
    />
    <figcaption>
      <span class="legend">
        <span class="key"><span class="swatch core"></span> Zone 8a core</span>
        <span class="key"><span class="swatch present"></span> Where 8a also appears</span>
      </span>
      Approximate. Zone 8a runs in bands inside the shaded states, not edge to edge. It is
      densest across the coastal Southeast and turns up in pockets of the Southwest and
      Pacific Northwest, with smaller patches elsewhere. For exact boundaries, check the{' '}
      <a href="https://planthardiness.ars.usda.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official USDA Plant Hardiness Zone Map</a>.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    If you are a zone or two off, the rotation still works. You shift the dates to your own
    frost calendar and keep the spacing intact. More on that below.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Zone 8a hands you a long shoulder season. Where I garden, the first hard frost holds off
    until around November 10, which is enough runway to grow three crops in the same bed once
    you stop treating it as a single planting and start treating it as a relay.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spring spinach goes in early and comes out by late spring. Summer bush beans follow and
    fix nitrogen, rebuilding what the spinach drew down. Then the fall and winter brassicas:
    broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, kale, collards. They mature into the cold, which is the
    whole point, because brassicas taste better after a frost. The handoffs are where this
    lives or dies. Get them right and the heavy-feeding brassicas inherit a bed that is ready
    for them instead of one you are scrambling to rescue.
  </p>

  <h2>What is on the site</h2>
  <p>
    Six guides, grouped into three stages: Plan, Plant, and Protect.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A <a href={`${base}/guides/planting-calendar/`}>master planting calendar</a> of eleven
      crops with varieties, start and sow dates, days to maturity, and harvest windows, all
      keyed to a November 10 frost date.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <a href={`${base}/guides/bed-layout/`}>polyculture bed layout</a> in three tiers by
      height and light, so a 4x8 bed pulls the weight of a bigger one.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <a href={`${base}/guides/succession-sequence/`}>succession and soil method</a>: the
      chop-and-drop handoff and the shallow manure amendment that move the bed cleanly from one
      crop to the next.
    </li>
    <li>
      An <a href={`${base}/guides/pest-management/`}>integrated pest management plan</a> for
      the August transplant window, when brassica pests are at their worst. Row covers, a Bt
      spray schedule, trap crops, and a homemade insecticidal soap recipe.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <a href={`${base}/guides/companion-planting/`}>companion planting rules</a>,
      including the one rule with no exceptions: keep fennel out of the bed entirely.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why you can trust it</h2>
  <p>
    Let me be plain about sourcing. The calendar and bed layout come from a verified working
    plan I have actually run. The pest and soil guidance is written to be checked: the soap
    concentrations, the warnings against dish-soap and kitchen brews, and the caterpillar
    controls all cite university extension and IPM publications, from Colorado State, UC IPM,
    Maryland, Minnesota, Clemson, and Penn State. Where a step was reconstructed from standard
    Zone 8a practice rather than a single authority, the site says so and tells you to confirm
    product labels and local extension guidance before you spray. I would rather tell you what
    is solid and what to double-check than pretend it is all settled.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone in Zone 8a who wants a complete, opinionated rotation they can copy this season, and
    anyone in a nearby zone who can move the dates to their own frost calendar. The dates are
    the only part tied to my particular patch of ground. The rest is built to be lifted whole.
  </p>

  <p>
    Start with the <a href={`${base}/guides/overview/`}>overview</a>, then walk the calendar,
    the layout, and the pest plan in that order. If you run a raised bed somewhere with cold
    winters and mild shoulders, I think this one will travel.
  </p>

  <p>
    Built and maintained by <a href="https://blanewarrene.com">Blane Warrene</a>. The site is a
    static build; if you are curious how it is put together, there is a link in the footer.
  </p>

  <style>
    .zone-map { margin: 1.75rem 0; }
    .zone-map img {
      width: 100%;
      height: auto;
      border: 1px solid var(--line);
      border-radius: 4px;
      background: #fff;
      display: block;
    }
    .zone-map figcaption {
      font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: #6b6f63;
      margin-top: 0.65rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
    }
    .zone-map .legend {
      display: flex;
      gap: 1.25rem;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }
    .zone-map .key { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem; }
    .zone-map .swatch {
      width: 0.9rem;
      height: 0.9rem;
      border-radius: 2px;
      display: inline-block;
      border: 1px solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
    }
    .zone-map .swatch.core { background: #2c4d28; }
    .zone-map .swatch.present { background: #7aa06e; }
  </style>
</Base>
